[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The test runner dynamically loads the compiled Rust shared library ([38;5;10mlibblake.so[0m) and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m. The Rust translation exported the function as just [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m, causing a [38;5;10mdlsym[0m failure at runtime.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
In the C code, the function name goes through a namespace macro:[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10m// hash.h
#define prf_addr SPX_NAMESPACE(prf_addr)

// params.h  
#define SPX_NAMESPACE(s) SPX_##s
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
So the actual C symbol name after preprocessing is [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m, not [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m. The LLM that produced the translation missed this macro expansion.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## What I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One-line fix in [38;5;10mtranslated_rust/src/lib.rs[0m — renamed the [38;5;10m#[no_mangle][0m exported function from [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m to [38;5;10mSPX_prf_addr[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m// Before:
pub extern "C" fn prf_addr(

// After:
pub extern "C" fn SPX_prf_addr(
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The BLAKE-256 implementation and the [38;5;10mprf_addr[0m logic itself were correct — only the exported symbol name was wrong.[0m[0m
